<h1>Method and sources</h1>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Not affiliated with NHTSA, the U.S. Department of Transportation, or any automaker.</strong> This is an independent site that counts NHTSA's public records. Brand and model names belong to their owners and are used only to say which vehicle a record is about.</p></div>

<h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
<p>Every number comes from two free public APIs run by the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration (<a href="https://www.nhtsa.gov/nhtsa-datasets-and-apis" rel="nofollow noopener">NHTSA datasets and APIs</a>):</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Complaints</strong>: <code>https://api.nhtsa.gov/complaints/complaintsByVehicle?make=…&amp;model=…&amp;modelYear=…</code></li>
<li><strong>Recalls</strong>: <code>https://api.nhtsa.gov/recalls/recallsByVehicle?make=…&amp;model=…&amp;modelYear=…</code></li>
</ul>
<p>A script (<code>scripts/fetch-nhtsa.mjs</code> in the <a href="{{ site.repo }}">public repository</a>) calls both APIs for each model year, saves the answers as JSON in the repository, and the site is built only from those saved files. For a recall that shows a potential unit count, the same script also calls <code>https://api.nhtsa.gov/recalls/campaignNumber?campaignNumber=…</code>. Each page links the exact API URLs it was built from and shows the date they were checked. The newest file in the cache was checked {{ nhtsa.checked | prettyDate }}. A page can show an older date when that model was not in the latest fetch.</p>

<h2>How the numbers are counted</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Complaints</strong> is the number of complaint records NHTSA returns for that make, model and model year.</li>
<li><strong>Component groups</strong> are NHTSA's own labels, chosen by the person filing. A complaint can name several, so a page's shares add up to more than 100%. We rejoin labels that contain a comma (for example "Service brakes, hydraulic") and fold child-car-seat parts into one "Child seat" group. The one-line descriptions under each label are ours.</li>
<li><strong>Crash, fire, injuries, deaths</strong> are the flags and counts on the complaint records, added up. They are what owners reported, not NHTSA findings.</li>
<li><strong>Recalls</strong> are the campaigns NHTSA's recall API returns for that model year. The summary, risk and remedy text on each recall is NHTSA's own wording, shown unchanged and labelled as such.</li>
<li><strong>Complaint-count order</strong> compares model years of the same model, 2010–2020, by the number of owner complaints. It is not a reliability or safety ranking, and it is not adjusted for sales or age. This site does not publish a best-year or worst-year list.</li>
<li><strong>Component pages</strong> follow one NHTSA component group across the model years of one model. They are published only when that group is the one owners named most often in several years and the page can show the matching recall notices separately from the complaint counts. See <code>NIGHTLY.md</code> in the repository for the evidence bar.</li>
<li><strong>Possible gaps</strong>: if the API returns no complaints for a year that has recalls, or under 10% of both neighbouring years, the year is highlighted as a likely gap in the API's answer and left out of the complaint-count order.{% for m in nhtsa.models %}{% for y in m.years %}{% if y.suspectGap %} Flagged now: {{ y.year }} {{ m.name }} ({{ y.s.total | n }} complaints returned, {{ y.s.recalls.length }} recalls).{% endif %}{% endfor %}{% endfor %}</li>
</ul>

<h2>What the numbers can't tell you</h2>
<ul>
<li>NHTSA does not verify complaints. Some are duplicates, some are wrong about the cause.</li>
<li>Counts are not divided by sales. A best-seller collects more complaints than a rare car without being worse per car.</li>
<li>Older model years have had longer to collect complaints; the newest years look better partly because they are young.</li>
<li>A recall listed for a model year may cover only some of those cars. Your VIN lookup at <a href="https://www.nhtsa.gov/recalls" rel="nofollow noopener">nhtsa.gov/recalls</a> is the final word for your car.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What we don't do</h2>
<ul>
<li>We don't publish complaint narratives. They are owners' own words and can include personal details; we only publish counts.</li>
<li>We don't copy other car-problem sites, invent numbers, write reviews, or rate cars.</li>
<li>No affiliate links (see <a href="/disclosure/">disclosure</a>) and no ads today.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Which models are covered</h2>
<p>We started with best-selling Honda, Toyota, Ford, Chevrolet and Nissan models, model years 2010–2020. Every model has a page comparing all its years. Full model-year pages start with the year each model has the most complaints, then further high-count years; one more page is added most nights. Corrections: <a href="mailto:{{ site.contact }}">{{ site.contact }}</a>.</p>
